+# 0018 — The endpoint key travels by the name it resolved from
+
+Status: accepted. 2026-11-04.
+
+## Context
+
+#65: a user set `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` and not the configured `OPENAI_API_KEY`.
+The parent session worked, because `secrets::api_key` falls back from the
+configured name to `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`; a delegated child got a 401 `No cookie
+auth credentials found`, because it was handed only the configured name and
+`env_key` has no fallback by design.
+
+The same shape of problem without a delegation: with no key at all, construct
+sent the request unauthenticated and showed the provider's 401 body, which does
+not say "no key was found".
+
+ADR 0014 settled that credentials follow the endpoint: a child gets its
+definition's `api_key_secret`, or the parent's when the definition doesn't name
+one. That stays. What it did not settle is *which name* the parent's key has
+when it came from the fallback.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `secrets::api_key` returns the key **with the name it resolved from**
+  (`secrets::Key { name, value }`), the configured secret first, then
+  `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` (`secrets::FALLBACK_KEY`).
+- A definition with no `api_key_secret` of its own, on the parent's endpoint,
+  gets the parent's key under that resolved name: `AgentDef::key_secret` writes
+  the resolved name into the child's config, and `secret_names` passes it in the
+  child's environment. The child reads `env_key(cfg.api_key_secret)` — the same
+  name it was handed — and still has no fallback of its own, so nothing the
+  parent did not hand it is visible to it.
+- A missing key fails before the first request, in both processes: the parent
+  (`secrets::require_key`) and the child (`secrets::require_env_key`) return
+  `no API key: set OPENAI_API_KEY or OPENROUTER_API_KEY (or construct secrets set
+  OPENAI_API_KEY)`, and the learning pass's clients use the same check.
+- `api_key_secret = ""` is an endpoint that takes no key: no lookup, no
+  fallback, no error, and no key name passed to a child.
+- A child that can't start writes the reason as an `Event::Error` before it
+  exits, so the parent's delegate failure carries "no API key: …" rather than
+  the provider's 401 or a bare exit status.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Easy: any way the parent resolves its key, the child resolves the same one, and
+a misconfiguration is one clear sentence instead of a provider's auth error.
+Hard: the child's environment key name now depends on the parent's environment,
+so a definition that pins `api_key_secret` still relies on the parent setting
+that exact name (it always did). Revisit if a definition needs a key for a
+different host under a second name; that is `secrets = [...]` plus
+`api_key_secret`.
+
+## Evidence
+
+#65. `crates/construct-dev/tests/delegate.rs`: with only `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`
+set, the child's request carries `Authorization: Bearer or-k…`; with no key,
+nothing reaches the endpoint and the run fails with the message above; a child
+with an unset `api_key_secret` reports "no API key: set CHILD_KEY" instead of a
+401; `api_key_secret = ""` sends no `Authorization` header.

